      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To report the long-term follow-up of a cohort of adult patients with supratentorial low-grade glioma treated at a single institution."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND MATERIALS",
          "content": "A cohort of 145 adult patients treated at the London Regional Cancer Program between 1979 and 1995 was reviewed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "With a median follow-up of 105 months, the median progression-free survival was 61 months (95% confidence interval, 53-77), and the median overall survival was 118 months (95% confidence interval, 93-129). The 10- and 20-year progression-free and overall survival rate was 18% and 0% and 48% and 22%, respectively. Cox regression analysis confirmed the importance of age, histologic type, presence of seizures, Karnofsky performance status, and initial extent of surgery as prognostic variables for overall and cause-specific survival. Function among long-term survivors without tumor progression was good to excellent for most patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Low-grade glioma is a chronic disease, with most patients dying of their disease. However, long-term survival with good function is possible. Survival is determined primarily by the disease factors with selection and timing of adjuvant treatments having less influence on outcome."
        }
      ]},
